full_judgment:
Md Muzammel Hossain CJ: This petition for leave to appeal under Article 103 of the Constitution of the People&#39;s Republic of Bangladesh is directed against the judgment and order dated 13-1-2011 passed by the High Court Division in Writ Petition No. 2252 of 2010 making the Rule absolute with a direction upon the writ respondents to transfer the writ petitioners service as computer operators to the revenue budget of the Bangladesh Technical Education Board with effect from 1-7-2005 within 2(two) months from the date of receipt of the judgment provided that the writ petitioners would not get any further salary for the period they had served on contract basis for which they had already been paid.&nbsp; 

 2. The present respondent Nos. 1-3 as petitioners filed Writ Petition No. 2252 of 2010 before the High Court Division stating, inter alia, that the writ petitioner No. 1 passed BA Examination in the year 1996 from Gouranadi Government College, Barisal and that the writ petitioner No. 2 passed BSc Examination in the year 1998 from Sardah College, Rajshahi and the writ petitioner No. 3 passed MSc Examination in the year 2000 from Jagannath University College, Dhaka. They were appointed on 9-6-2004 as Computer Operators in the scale of Taka 4,125 (250-5505) in the Project for Computerization of Examination Activities of Bangladesh Technical Education Board (BTEB) pursuant to Notification No. বাকাশিবো/এম.আই.এস/২০০৪/১৫৩(৬) dated 9-6-2004 issued by the Bangladesh Technical Education Board. The writ petitioner Nos. 1, 2 and 3 joined on 14-6-2004, 24-6-2004 and 12-6-2004 respectively in their service. The writ petitioners continued their job till June, 2005 on the basis of the above mentioned appointment and at the end of the Project the petitioners were allowed to retain their posts as Computer Operators on contractual basis for a period of 6(six) months each time till June, 2009 vide letters dated 30-6-2005, 22-1-2006, 24-7-2006, 28-12-2006, 25-7-2007, 31-12-2008 and 22-1-2009 issued by the writ respondent No. 1 petitioner with commitment that they would be appointed to their posts on regular basis on the revenue head of the Government as soon as sanctions for the said posts is obtained from the government. The service of the writ petitioner No. 2 on contractual basis was discontinued since 25-7-2007 but he was verbally allowed to work on daily basis till June, 2009.&nbsp; 

 3. The Ministry of Education vide Memo No. শিম/শাঃ১৬(প্রজেক্ট)৬-২-/২০০৫/(অংশ-১)/৩২ dated 30-1-2008 (Annexure-C) sanctioned 9(nine) posts of the Project for computerization of examination activities of the Board and out of the 9(nine) posts 4(four) posts were Computer Operators. The writ petitioners stated that clause 49 of the Project Profile (Annexure-D) contemplates that after the life time of the project the manpower (ten persons) of the Computerization of the project would be transferred to the revenue budget of the Board for the public interest and for continuing the computerization of examination activities in BTEB. But out of 4 sanctioned revenue posts of Computer Operators, only in one post a Computer Operator of the Project was appointed and in three other posts, persons from outside were appointed on the basis of Memo No. বাকাশিবো/প্রাঃ/০৮/২০১/২০১০/১৫২২ dated 23-2-2010 (Annexure-E) and by Memo No. বাকাশিবো/প্রাঃ/০৮/২০১/২০১০/১৫২২ dated 23-2-2010 (Annexure-G) only one post of Despatch Rider of the Board who was in service on contract basis was absorbed and appointed in the revenue posts. Thus, to the great mental shock and dismay, the writ petitioners were&nbsp; illegally left out of the appointment denying them the equal treatment and frustrating their legitimate expectation for appointment to the sanctioned revenue posts pursuant to clause 49 of the Project Profile. The writ petitioners further asserted that the Notification vide SRO No. 182 /Ain/2005 Shama/Bidhi-1 /S-9/2000 dated 20-6-2005 published in Extra-ordinary Gazette dated 20-6-2005 concerning regularization and fixing seniority of the persons who have been transferred to the revenue set up from development project is not applicable in the case of the writ petitioners&#39; service since they were appointed in the Project on 9-6-2004 prior to the publication of the said Notification and as per Rule 2(ka) of the Rules of 2005 contained by the aforesaid Notification &quot;development project&quot; means any project approved by the government under development budget started between 9th April, 1972 to 30th June, 1997.&nbsp; 

 4. On 23-7-2007, 27-7-2008 and 29-7-2008 the writ respondents issued certificates of good performance [Annexure-F, F(1) and F(2)] respectively in favour of the writ petitioners and under such circumstances, having requisite qualifications, the writ petitioners rendered commendable service to the Board for a long period and that they have got legitimate expectation that they would be appointed to the vacant regular posts of Computer Operators with grade and scale as mentioned in Memo No. শিম/শাঃ১৬/বোর্ড (প্রজেক্ট)৬-২/২০০৫/(অংশ-১)/৩২ dated 30-1-2008 (Annexure-C) creating 9(nine) posts under the revenue set up of the Board. There are instances and age old practice for the Board&#39;s employees to be absorbed and appointed in permanent posts from contractual service and recently also one Md Faruque, son of Md Moinuddin Sardar, a Despatch Rider of the Board on contractual basis was absorbed and appointed to the revenue post vide Memo No. বাকাশিবো/প্রাঃ/০৮/২০১/২০১০/১৫২২ dated 23-2- 2010. The writ respondent No. 2 on 6-7-2003 issued public advertisement vide Memo No. বাকাশিবো/প্রাঃ/২০০৮/৮১২ dated 6-7-2008 inviting applications for appointment to&nbsp; various permanent posts including 3(three) Computer Operators under the Board, but still now, no appointment to the permanent vacant posts of Computer Operators has been made by the Board and these posts are lying vacant with the Board.&nbsp; 

 5. A Division Bench of the High Court Division after hearing both parties by the impugned judgment and order dated 13-1-2011 made the Rule absolute with direction.&nbsp; 

 6. The present petitioner being aggrieved by the impugned judgment and order dated 13-1-2011 passed by the High Court Division preferred the instant Civil Petition for Leave to Appeal before this Division.&nbsp; 

 7. Mr AFM Mesbahuddin, the learned Advocate appearing on behalf of the respondents- petitioners submits that the High Court Division did not consider the facts that the Bangladesh Technical Education Board sent the proposal to the Ministry of Education for transfer of the writ petitioners&#39; service to the revenue setup, but the Ministry of Establishment rejected the proposal and as such they have no authority to transfer the petitioner&#39;s service to the revenue setup. He submits that the High Court Division ignored the facts that the respondent Nos. 1-3-Writ petitioners applied for and appeared in the competency tests for direct appointment and thus they waived their right if any and, as such, the judgment and order of the High Court Division with direction is liable to be set-aside. He further submits that the High Court Division on misconception of law relying upon clause 49 of the project profile held that the petitioners were promise bound to transfer the service of the respondent Nos. 1-3 to the revenue budget of the Board in public interest at the end of the project. He finally submits that the High Court Division overlooked that the project namely computerization of Examination Activities of Bangladesh Technical Education Board started for the period of 2(two) years from July, 2002 to June, 2004 and after completion of the project the petitioners sent proposal to the Ministry to absorb the respondent Nos. 1-3 but the Ministry refused and, as such, the impugned judgment and order passed by the High Court Division is liable to be set-aside.&nbsp; 

 8. On the contrary the respondent Nos. 1-3 appearing in persons contended that there is no illegality in the impugned judgment and order passed by the High Court Division because the writ-respondent-petitioner appointed the respondent Nos. 1 to 3 with the condition that after the life time of the project their service will be transferred to the revenue setup. Clause 49 of the Project Profile contemplates that after the life time of the project the man-power of the computerization of the project will be transferred to revenue setup of the Board for the public interest and for continuing the computerization of examination activities in the Board.&nbsp; 

 9. We have heard the learned Advocate for both the parties and perused the civil petition for leave to appeal, impugned judgment and order of the High Court Division and other materials on record.&nbsp; 

 11. On perusal of Annexure-B series it appears that at the end of the Project the respondent Nos. 1-3 were retained in their respective posts as Computer Operators on contractual basis for a period of 6(six) months in each time till June, 2009 vide letters dated 30-6-2005, 22-1-2006, 24-7-2006, 28-12-2006, 25-7-2007, 31-12-2007, 15-7-2008 and 22-1-2009 issued by the petitioner. Though the service of the respondent No. 2 on contractual basis was discontinued on 25-7-2007 yet he was verbally allowed to work on daily basis till June, 2009. Earlier Ministry of Establishment by its Memo dated 27-4-2005 (Annexure-1) rejected the proposal of the Ministry of Education vide Memo dated 29-3-2005 to transfer the 10 posts of the Computerization of the Project to the revenue budget.&nbsp; 

 14. From the above discussions, we find that apart from the stipulations made in clause 49 of the Project Profile to the effect that after the completion of the Project the manpower of the Computer Cell of the Project will be transferred to revenue budget of the Bangladesh Technical Education Board for the public interest, the petitioners in the meeting of the Board on 8-1-2008 decided to re-appoint the employees who were earlier appointed on contractual basis for smooth functioning of the Computerization of Examination Activities of the Board vide Memo dated 8-1-2008. In this context it is pertinent to quote clause 49 of the Project Profile which reads as under:&nbsp; 

 &quot;After the life time of the project the manpower (ten persons) of the computer cell of the project will be transferred to revenue budget of Bangladesh Technical Education Board for the public interest and for continuing of the computerization of examination activities in BTEB.&quot;&nbsp; 

 15. Sanction for 9 posts of the Project for Computerization of Examination Activities of Bangladesh Technical Education Board was awarded on temporary basis by the Ministry of Education vide Memo dated 30-1-2008 (Annexure-C) with the approval of the Ministry of Establishment and the Ministry of Finance and out of 9 posts there were 4 (four) posts of Computer Operators along with 5(five) other posts and it was decided that the payment of the aforesaid posts will be made from revenue budget.&nbsp; 

 16. It appears that the petitioner published recruitment notice vide Memo dated 6-7-2008 (Annexure-H) inviting applications amongst others for 3(three) posts of Computer Operators. Thereafter upon interview and recommendation of the Selection Committee one Md. Shahadat Hossain a computer operator of the Project was appointed in one out of 4(four) sanctioned posts and in other three posts, persons from outside the Project were appointed vide Memo dated 23-2-2010 (Annexure-E) but the respondents were left out of the appointment to the regular sanctioned revenue posts of Computer Operators.&nbsp; 

 17. It appears that the notification issued vide SRO No. 182/Ain/2005/Shama/Bidhi-1 /S-9 /2000 dated 20-6-2005 published in Extraordinary Gazette concerning regularization and fixing seniority of the persons who have been transferred to the revenue setup from development project is not applicable to the writ petitioners because as per Rule 2(ka) these Rules are meant only for those development projects which started between 9th April, 1972 to 30th June, 1997.&nbsp; 

 18. In the instant case before us the respondent Nos. 1-3 were appointed as Computer Operators under the Project for computerization of examination activities of the Bangladesh Technical Education Board pursuant to notification dated 9-6-2004. We have noticed that clause 49 of the Project Profile unequivocally provided that after the conclusion of the Project the manpower of the computer cell of the project will be transferred to the revenue budget of the Board. It is true that there is no provision for re-appointment of the manpower of the computer cell when a project is completed. But clause 49 provides for transfer of them to the revenue budget after the completion of the Project. It has been asserted on behalf of the petitioner that the Ministry of Establishment rejected the proposal of the Ministry of Education for transfer of the respondents&#39; service to the revenue setup. But this does not improve the case of the petitioner in any way. Because the petitioner made representation to the respondent Nos. 1 to 3 that their service would be transferred to the revenue budget after completion of the project.&nbsp; 

 19. The writ petitioners acted upon this representation to their detriment by not seeking other employment after completion of the project. Issuance of certificate of good performance in favour of the writ petitioners by the writ respondents [Annexures-F, F(1) and F(2)] together with their continuous appointment on contractual basis for a period of 6 months each time till June, 2009 gave high hope and created legitimate expectation among them that their job would be absorbed in the revenue setup. We have also seen that after completion of the Project the respondent Nos.1-3 were appointed as Computer Operators on contractual basis for smooth functioning of the Computerization of Examination Activities of Bangladesh Technical Education Board till June, 2009. We have seen that service of the respondent No. 2 though discontinued on 25-7-2007 yet he was verbally allowed to work on daily basis till June 2009. It also appears that the Ministry of Education vide Memo dated 30-1-2008 (Annexure-C) sanctioned 9(nine) posts of the Project for computerization of Examination Activities of Bangladesh Technical Education Board including 4(four) posts of Computer Operators of the Board on temporary basis with the approval of the Ministry of Establishment and the Ministry of Finance. But out of 4(four) sanctioned revenue posts of Computer Operators, only one person, Md Shahadat Hossain, a computer operator of the Project was appointed in the revenue budget and the respondent Nos. 1-3 were left out on the alleged ground that they failed to qualify themselves to be appointed under the revenue budget and, as such, they could not be appointed.&nbsp; 

 20. From the facts of the case it appears that 3(three) posts are lying vacant but the service of the respondent Nos. 1-3 were not transferred under the revenue budget. As per clause 49 of the Project Profile respondent Nos. 1-3 made representation to the petitioner that after the completion of the Project their service should be transferred under the revenue budget without any examination for selection to those posts. The respondent Nos. 1-3 having relied on clause 49 joined in the service under the Project and had worked there till the completion of the Project and after completion of the Project the petitioner was obliged to appoint the respondent Nos. 1-3 under the revenue budget and, as such, the petitioners are estopped by the doctrine of promissory estoppel to appoint anyone other than the respondent Nos. 1 to 3 under the revenue budget. When a person makes a representation to any other person to do something and in reliance of which representation, the represent or changed his position to his detriment, the represent or cannot go back from his representation. This Court in a number of cases held that represented will be estopped by the doctrine of promissory estoppel to retract from going back from his commitment.&nbsp; 

 23. The Supreme Court of India in the case of M/s. Motilal Padampat Sugar Mills Co. Ltd. vs State of Uttar Pradesh, AIR 1979 (SC) 621 at page 644 observed as under:&nbsp; 

 &quot;But it is necessary to point out that since the doctrine of promissory estoppel is an equitable doctrine, it must yield when the equity so requires. If it can be shown by the Government that having regard to the facts as they have subsequently transpired, it would be inequitable to hold the Government to the promise made by it, the Court would not raise equity in favour of the promise and enforce the promise against the Government.&quot;&nbsp; 

 28. A substantive legitimate expectation is based on a promise or representation about an actual advantage or benefit. The Hong Kong Court of Appeal defined the substantive legitimate expectation doctrine in the following terms:&nbsp; 

 &quot;The doctrine recognizes that, in the absence of any overriding reason of law or policy excluding its operation, situations may arise in which persons may have a legitimate expectation of a substantive out-come or benefit, in which event failing to honour the expectation may, in particular circumstances, result in such unfairness to individuals as to amount to an abuse of power justifying intervention by the court.&quot; [Tung vs Director of Immigration (2002) 1 HKLRD 561, 600]&nbsp; 

 &quot;60. The principle of legitimate expectation as enunciated in Schmidt vs Secretary of State of Home Affairs reported in (1969) I Ch. 140 imposed upon the Government or the concerned Authorities a duty to act fairly and reasonably in dealing with the rights and/or interest of the people and in case of any breach thereof the Court should act upon in striking down any order in exercise of judicial review of executive action and in case of any unreasonableness or inertness or even latches in its part for which some or part of which benefit is lost causing prejudice to the people at large, the Court may direct the concerned to exercise its functions fairly, reasonably and in accordance with law and established principles.&quot;&nbsp; 

 32. The Supreme Court of India while explaining the nature and scope of the doctrine of legitimate expectation in the case of Food Corporation of India vs M/s Kamdhenu Cattle Feed Industries (1993) 1 SCC 71 at page 76, paragraph 8 observed as under:&nbsp; 

 &quot;32 A case for applicability of the doctrine of legitimate expectation, now accepted in the subjective sense as part of our legal jurisprudence, arises when an administrative body by reason of a representation or by past practice or conduct aroused an expectation which it would be within its powers to fulfill unless some overriding public interest comes in the way. However, a person who bases his claim on the doctrine of legitimate expectation, in the first instance, has to satisfy that he has relied on the said representation and the denial of that expectation has worked to his detriment. The Court could interfere only if the decision taken by the authority was found to be arbitrary, unreasonable or in gross abuse of power or in violation of principles of natural justice and not taken in public interest. But a claim based on mere legitimate expectation without anything more cannot ipso facto give a right to invoke these principles.&quot;&nbsp; 

 34. The present respondent as writ petitioners laid a foundation in the petition that they were appointed in the Project with an understanding that after the life time of the Project their service will be transferred to the revenue budget of Bangladesh Technical Education Board for the public interest and continuing of the Computerization of Examination Activities of Bangladesh Technical Education Board pursuant to clause 49 of the Project Profile. Thereby the writ petitioners successfully made out a case of their service to be transferred to the revenue setup on the doctrine of legitimate expectation. From a plain reading of clause 49 of the Project Profile it is evident that Bangladesh Technical Education Board made a representation to the writ petitioners that after the completion of Project their service would be transferred to revenue budget of Bangladesh Technical Education Board. Thus the writ petitioners were assured that they would be regularized in the revenue setup after the completion of the Project. In the instant case the writ petitioners having requisite qualifications appeared before the competent Selection Committee who after proper examination through a competitive examination among the prospective candidates found the writ petitioners to be competent and qualified and appointed them in the Project. After the life time of the Project the writ petitioners were not transferred to the revenue setup pursuant to clause 49 of the Project Profile. For the reason best known to the writ respondent No. 1 petitioner at the end of the Project the writ petitioners were allowed to retain in their respective posts as Computer Operators on contractual basis till June 2009 after completion of the project. It has been noticed that the writ respondent No. 1-petitioner made representation/commitment that they will be appointed to their posts on regular basis in the revenue setup of the Government as soon as sanction for the said posts is obtained from the Government. On perusal of the Annexure B series it appears that the writ petitioners-respondent Nos. 1 to 3 were appointed on temporary basis for a period of six months and their services were renewed after the expiry of every six months till 22-1-2009. It is true that the writ petitioners after completion of the Project continued their service on contractual basis and they also faced interview for direct appointment in those posts but failed to succeed in the interview. Since the writ petitioners were assured that pursuant to clause 49 of the Project Profile after life time of the Project their service would be transferred to the revenue budget rather their service were continued on contractual basis till 2009. The writ petitioners-respondent Nos. 1 to 3 in a compelling circumstance having no other alternative continued their service on contractual basis after completion of their Project and thereby they cannot be termed to have waved their right of being absorbed in the revenue setup under the Government.&nbsp; 

 35. It also appears that the respondent Nos. 1-3 successfully completed 5(five) years of their service to the Board with dedication and sincerity. It is their legitimate expectation that after the completion of the Project their service will be transferred to the revenue setup pursuant to clause 49 of the Project Profile. We find that the High Court Division rightly found that the respondent Nos. 1 to 3 having appeared before the Interview Board for direct appointment to the post of Computer Operators of the Board have not waved their right to be transferred to the revenue setup of the Board. From Annexure-B series of the writ petition we find that the respondent Nos. 1 to 3 continued in their service till June, 2005 on the basis of their earlier appointment and that the Project ended on 30-6-2005. Therefore, the respondent Nos. 1-3 were legally entitled to have their service transferred to the revenue budget of the Board on and from 1-7-2005. We do not find any merit in the submission of the learned Advocate appearing for the petitioner.&nbsp; 

 36. In the above facts and circumstances, we are of the view that the High Court Division having considered all the materials on record and the submissions advanced on behalf of both the parties arrived at a correct decision in making the Rule absolute with a direction upon the petitioner-Board to transfer the respondents&#39; service as Computer Operators to the revenue budget with effect from 1-7-2005 within 2(two) months from the date of receipt of the judgment. However, the writ-petitioners-respondents would not get any extra salary for the period they served on contractual basis which does not require any interference by this Division.&nbsp;
